.TH fcd 1 "Oct 2026" "fcd 2.0.0" "fcd - an interactive directory navigator"

.SH NAME
fcd - an interactive directory navigator powered by fzf

.SH SYNOPSIS
fcd [\fIoptions\fR]

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBfcd\fR is an interactive directory navigator powered by
.BR fzf (1).
It provides an interactive interface for browsing directories, mounted drives
and partitions, and saved bookmarks, with fuzzy search and configurable
previews, sorting, path display, Git information, clipboard integration, and
tmux integration.

fcd has three navigation modes:

.br
* \fBdir\fR browses directories from the current directory or the path given by \fB\-\-walker\-root\fR
.br
* \fBdrive\fR lists mounted drives and partitions detected with \fBlsblk\fR
.br
* \fBbookmark\fR lists saved bookmarks from the configured bookmark file

.SH OPTIONS
.SS NOTE
.TP
After a target is selected, \fBfcd\fR prints its path to standard output.
It does not change the current directory of the calling shell by itself.
Shell integration or a shell wrapper can use the printed path to change
directories.

.SS OUTPUT
.TP
.B "\-\-print0"
Print output delimited by ASCII NUL characters instead of newline characters
.TP
.B "\-\-print\-current"
Print the target directory even when it is the directory from which
.B fcd
was started. Without this option, selecting the starting directory is treated
as a no-op.
.TP
.B "\-\-print\-current\-dot"
Print \(dq.\(dq when the selected target is the directory from which
.B fcd
was started. Selecting the starting directory still returns the current-directory
status described in the \fBEXIT STATUS\fR section.

.SS NAVIGATION
.TP
.BI "\-\-walker=" "OPTS"
Select the navigation mode and traversal behavior. The value is a
comma-separated list containing one of the navigation modes and optional
visibility and symbolic-link settings.

.RS
.B dir
.RS
Browse directories. This is the default navigation mode.
.RE
.B drive
.RS
List mounted drives and partitions detected through
.BR lsblk (8).
Entries without a mount point are ignored, and swap, encrypted,
and LVM member devices are excluded. The standard system mount points
(\fB/\fR, \fB/home\fR, \fB/boot\fR, \fB/boot/efi\fR, and \fB/var\fR) are combined
into a single \fBSystem\fR entry.
.RE
.B bookmark
.RS
Browse saved bookmarked directories.
.RE
.B follow
.RS
Follow symbolic links during directory traversal. This is enabled by default.
.RE
.B nofollow
.RS
Do not follow symbolic links during directory traversal.
.RE
.B hidden
.RS
Include hidden directories in the navigation list.
.RE
.B nohidden
.RS
Hide hidden directories in the navigation list. This is enabled by default.
.RE
.RE

.RS
The default value is
.BR dir,follow,nohidden .
When using this option, the list must contain \fBexactly one\fR primary navigation mode
(\fBdir\fR, \fBdrive\fR, or \fBbookmark\fR).
.RE

.TP
.BI "\-\-walker\-root=" "DIR"
Set the root directory for directory navigation. The default is the current
working directory. The option is primarily meaningful in \fBdir\fR mode.

.TP
.BI "\-\-walker\-skip=" "DIRS"
Comma-separated list of directory names to skip during traversal. The
same skip list is used when generating directory previews. The default is
.BR .git,node_modules .

.TP
.BI "\-\-max\-depth=" "MAX_DEPTH"
Limit directory traversal to the specified positive integer depth. The default
is \fB1\fR.
.TP
.B "\-\-no\-max\-depth"
Disable the maximum directory traversal depth

.SS SORTING
.TP
.BI "\-\-sort" "[=ORDER]"
Enable sorting of directory entries. \fIORDER\fR can be \fBasc\fR (default) or \fBdesc\fR.
Since sorting is blocking (requires reading all matching entries before rendering),
it is recommended to disable it with
.B \-\-no\-sort
when using a large
.B \-\-max\-depth
to avoid hanging or latency.
.TP
.B "\-\-no\-sort"
Disable sorting of directory entries. Useful to speed up startup time and avoid delays
when traversing deep directory trees.
.TP
.BI "\-\-sort\-order=" "ORDER"
Deprecated. Use
.BI "\-\-sort " "[asc|desc]"
instead.

.SS DISPLAY MODE
.TP
.BI "\-\-layout=" "LAYOUT"
Choose the layout (default: top)

.br
.BR bottom "       Display from the bottom of the screen"
.br
.BR top "          Display from the top of the screen"
.br
.BR top\-list "     Display from the top with the list arranged above the input"
.br

Internally,
.B bottom
maps to the fzf
.B default
layout,
.B top
maps to
.BR reverse ,
and
.B top\-list
maps to
.BR reverse\-list .
.TP
.BI "\-\-height=" "[~][\-]HEIGHT[%]"
Display fcd window below the cursor with the given height instead of using
the full screen.

If a negative value is specified, the height is calculated as the terminal
height minus the given value.

e.g.
     \fBfcd \-\-height=\-1\fR

When prefixed with \fB~\fR, the height will be automatically determined in the
range according to the input size. You can combine \fB~\fR with a negative
value.

e.g.
     \fB# Will not take up 100% of the screen
     fcd \-\-height=~100%

     # Adapt to list size, up to terminal height minus 1
     fcd \-\-height=~\-1\fR

Adaptive height has the following limitations:
.br
* Cannot be used with top/bottom margin and padding given in percent size
.br
* Initial rendering is delayed until directory traversal completes or outputs
enough items to satisfy the calculated height

.TP
.BI "\-\-min\-height=" "HEIGHT[+]"
Minimum height when \fB\-\-height\fR is given as a percentage.
Add \fB+\fR to automatically increase the value according to the other
layout options so that the specified number of items are visible in the list
section. The underlying fzf default for percentage heights is \fB10+\fR.
Ignored when \fB\-\-height\fR is not specified or set as an absolute value.

.TP
.BI "\-\-margin=" "MARGIN"
Comma-separated expression for margins around the finder.
.br

.br
.RS
.BR TRBL "     Same margin for top, right, bottom, and left"
.br
.BR TB,RL "    Vertical, horizontal margin"
.br
.BR T,RL,B "   Top, horizontal, bottom margin"
.br
.BR T,R,B,L "  Top, right, bottom, left margin"
.br

.br
Each part can be given in absolute number or in percentage relative to the
terminal size with \fB%\fR suffix.
.br

.br
e.g.
     \fBfcd \-\-margin 10%
     fcd \-\-margin 1,5%\fR
.RE
.TP
.BI "\-\-padding=" "PADDING"
Comma-separated expression for padding inside the border. Padding is
distinguishable from margin only when a border is drawn.
.br

.br
e.g.
     \fBfcd \-\-margin 5% \-\-padding 5% \-\-border rounded\fR

.br
.RS
.BR TRBL "     Same padding for top, right, bottom, and left"
.br
.BR TB,RL "    Vertical, horizontal padding"
.br
.BR T,RL,B "   Top, horizontal, bottom padding"
.br
.BR T,R,B,L "  Top, right, bottom, left padding"
.br
.RE

.TP
.BI "\-\-border=" "STYLE"
Draw border around the finder.

.br
.BR rounded "       Border with rounded corners (default)"
.br
.BR sharp "         Border with sharp corners"
.br
.BR bold "          Border with bold lines"
.br
.BR double "        Border with double lines"
.br
.BR dashed "        Border with dashed lines and rounded corners"
.br
.BR block "         Border using block elements; suitable when using different background colors"
.br
.BR thinblock "     Border using legacy computing symbols; may not be displayed on some terminals"
.br
.BR horizontal "    Horizontal lines above and below the finder"
.br
.BR vertical "      Vertical lines on each side of the finder"
.br
.BR line "          Single line border (position automatically determined)"
.br
.BR top " (up)"
.br
.BR bottom " (down)"
.br
.BR left
.br
.BR right
.br
.BR none
.br

The default is normally
.BR top .
When using \fB\-\-popup\fR or \fB\-\-tmux\fR inside tmux, with no explicit tmux options, the
border can default to \fBnone\fR when the path is anchored in the header or is
hidden; otherwise the default remains \fBtop\fR.

\fBline\fR style draws a single separator line at the top when \fB\-\-height\fR
is used.

.TP
.BI "\-\-ellipsis=" "STR"
Ellipsis to show when line or path is truncated (default: '··')

.SS LIST SECTION
.TP
.B "\-\-highlight\-line"
Highlight the whole current line
.TP
.B "\-\-no\-highlight\-line"
Disable whole-line highlighting. This is the default.
.TP
.B "\-\-cycle"
Enable cyclic scroll. This is the default.
.TP
.B "\-\-no\-cycle"
Disable cyclic scroll
.TP
.BI "\-\-wrap" "[=MODE]"
Enable line wrap. \fIMODE\fR can be \fBchar\fR or \fBword\fR (default).
\fBword\fR mode wraps lines at word boundaries (spaces and tabs) instead of
at arbitrary character positions.
.TP
.B "\-\-no\-wrap"
Disable line wrap

.SS INPUT SECTION
.TP
.B "\-\-no\-input"
Disable and hide the input section
.TP
.BI "\-\-info=" "STYLE"
Determines the display style of the finder info. (e.g. match counter, loading indicator, etc.)

.BR default  "              On the left end of the horizontal separator"
.br
.BR right  "                On the right end of the horizontal separator"
.br
.BR hidden  "               Do not display finder info"
.br
.BR inline  "               After the prompt with the default prefix ' < '"
.br
.BR inline:PREFIX  "        After the prompt with a non-default prefix"
.br
.BR inline\-right "         On the right end of the prompt line"
.br
.BR inline\-right:PREFIX "  On the right end of the prompt line with a custom prefix"
.br

.TP
.B "\-\-no\-info"
A synonym for \fB\-\-info=hidden\fB

.TP
.BI "\-\-separator=" "STR"
The given string will be repeated to form the horizontal separator on the info
line (default: '─' or '-').

ANSI color codes are supported.

.TP
.B "\-\-no\-separator"
Hide the information-line separator

.SS PATH DISPLAY
.TP
.BI "\-\-path\-position" [=N[:top|bottom|header|none]]
Configure where the current navigation path is displayed. The value may
specify a column and an optional anchor, separated by a colon.

.br
.BR N "        Place the path at column \fIN\fR counted from the left"
.br
.BR \-N "       Place the path at column \fIN\fR counted from the right"
.br
.BR :top "     Place the path on the top border"
.br
.BR :bottom "  Place the path on the bottom border"
.br
.BR :header "  Place the path in the header"
.br
.BR :none "    Hide the path label"
.br

When no explicit position is given, fcd selects a path anchor and column
according to the current border and layout. The value
.BR 0
places the path at the center column.
.TP
.BI "\-\-path\-padding=" "N"
Number of spaces placed around the displayed path. The default is \fB1\fR.
The value must be a non-negative integer.
.TP
.BI "\-\-path\-truncate=" "MODE"
Choose which end of an overlong path is truncated. Supported values are
.BR start
and
.BR end .
The default is
.BR start .

.SS PREVIEW WINDOW
.TP
.BI "\-\-preview=" "OPTS"
Configure the preview contents and traversal. The value is a comma-separated
list containing one or both of
.BR dir
and
.BR file ,
plus optional visibility, symbolic-link, sorting, display-mode, and depth
settings.

.br
.BR dir "       Include directories in the preview"
.br
.BR file "      Include files in the preview"
.br
.BR follow "    Follow symbolic links. This is enabled by default."
.br
.BR nofollow "  Do not follow symbolic links"
.br
.BR sort "      Sort preview entries. This is enabled by default."
.br
.BR nosort "    Do not sort preview entries"
.br
.BR hidden "    Show hidden preview entries"
.br
.BR nohidden "  Hide hidden preview entries. This is the default."
.br
.BR auto "      Use the navigation hidden-entry setting for preview visibility"
.br
.BR asc "       Sort preview entries in ascending order"
.br
.BR desc "      Sort preview entries in descending order"
.br
.BR list "      Use list format. This is the default format."
.br
.BR tree "      Use tree format through"
.BR tree (1).
.br
.BR grid "      Use a compact grid format"
.br
.BR N "         Use the non-negative integer \fIN\fR as the maximum preview depth. A value of 0 means unlimited depth."
.br

At least one of
.BR dir
or
.B file
must be specified. The configured defaults are
.BR dir,follow,sort,asc,list,nohidden,1 .

.TP
.BI "\-\-preview\-window=" "[POSITION][,SIZE[%]][,border\-STYLE][,[no]wrap][,wrap\-word][,[no]follow][,[no]cycle][,[no]info][,[no]hidden][,+SCROLL[OFFSETS][/DENOM]][,~HEADER_LINES][,default][,<SIZE_THRESHOLD(ALTERNATIVE_LAYOUT)]"

.RS
.B POSITION:
    \fBup
    \fBdown
    \fBleft
    \fBright
    \fBnext

\fRDetermines the layout of the preview window. The default is \fBnocycle:nohidden:40%:right\fR.

* \fBnext\fR places the preview window adjacent to the input section, on
the list side: below the input in the top layout, above the input
in \fB\-\-layout=bottom\fR.

* If the argument contains \fB:hidden\fR, the preview window will be hidden by
default. Press \fBCtrl-P\fR to toggle the preview window.

* If size is given as \fB0\fR, preview window will not be visible, but fzf will still
execute the command in the background.

* Long lines are truncated by default. Line wrap can be enabled with
\fBwrap\fR flag. \fBwrap\-word\fR flag enables word-level wrapping, which
breaks lines at word boundaries instead of mid-word.

* Preview window will automatically scroll to the bottom when \fBfollow\fR
flag is set.

* Cyclic scrolling is enabled with \fBcycle\fR flag.

* To hide the scroll offset information on the top right corner, specify
\fBnoinfo\fR.

* To change the style of the border of the preview window, specify one of:

.RS
.br
.B border\-rounded (default)
.br
.B border\-sharp
.br
.B border\-bold
.br
.B border\-double
.br
.B border\-dashed
.br
.B border\-block
.br
.B border\-thinblock
.br
.B border\-horizontal
.br
.B border\-top
.br
.B border\-bottom
.br
.B border\-line
.br
.RE

* \fBborder\-line\fR draws a single separator line between the preview window and
the rest of the interface. See
.B \-\-border
for the description of the border styles.

* \fB[:+SCROLL[OFFSETS][/DENOM]]\fR determines the initial scroll offset of the
preview window.

  - \fBSCROLL\fR can be either a numeric integer or a single-field index expression that refers to a numeric integer or {n} to refer to the zero-based ordinal index of the current item.

  - The optional \fBOFFSETS\fR part is for adjusting the base offset. It should be given as a series of signed integers (\fB\-INTEGER\fR or \fB+INTEGER\fR).

  - The final \fB/DENOM\fR part is for specifying a fraction of the preview window height.

* \fB~HEADER_LINES\fR keeps the top N lines as the fixed header so that they
are always visible.

* \fBdefault\fR resets all options previously set to the fzf default.

.RS
e.g.
     \fB# Non-default scroll window positions and sizes
     fcd \-\-preview=dir \-\-preview\-window=up,30%
     fcd \-\-preview=file \-\-preview\-window=down,1

     # Display top 3 lines as the fixed header
     fcd \-\-preview\-window '~3'\fR
.RE

* You can specify an alternative set of options that are used only when the size
  of the preview window is below a certain threshold. Note that only one
  alternative layout is allowed.

.RS
e.g.
     \fBfcd \-\-preview 'file,nosort' \-\-preview\-window 'right,border\-left,<30(up,30%,border\-bottom)'\fR
.RE

.SS GIT
.TP
.BI "\-\-git" "[=OPTS]"
Display Git repository information in the preview label. When
.B \-\-git
is used without a value,
.BR head
is enabled by default.

.br
.BR head "       Show the current HEAD location, such as a branch, tag, or short commit"
.br
.BR state "      Show staged and unstaged state indicators. A \fB+\fR indicates staged changes and \fB*\fR indicates unstaged changes."
.br
.BR stash "      Show the stash indicator \fBS\fR"
.br
.BR untracked "  Show the untracked-files indicator \fB%\fR"
.br
.BR conflict "   Show the merge or rebase conflict indicator \fB!\fR"
.br
.BR top\-level "  Show Git information only at repository top-level directories"
.br

At least one of
.BR head ,
.BR state ,
.BR stash ,
.BR untracked ,
or
.B conflict
must be specified.

.TP
.B "\-\-no\-git"
Disable Git information in the preview label

.SS TMUX
.TP
.BI "\-\-tmux" "[=OPTS]"
Run \fBfcd\fR through \fBfzf\-tmux\fR(1). When
.IR OPTS
is supplied, its contents are passed directly to fzf\-tmux; otherwise fcd uses
fzf\-tmux with its default options. Arguments starting with \-\- are not consumed as
.IR OPTS
and are instead processed as fcd options. This option requires fzf\-tmux
to be installed and is ignored if fcd is not running inside a tmux session.

Available layout options:

.br
     \fB\-p\fR [W[%][,H[%]]]  Popup window (default: 50%)
.br
     \fB\-w\fR WIDTH[%]       Popup width
.br
     \fB\-h\fR HEIGHT[%]      Popup height
.br
     \fB\-x\fR COL            Popup x-coordinate
.br
     \fB\-y\fR ROW            Popup y-coordinate
.br
     \fB\-u\fR [HEIGHT[%]]    Split pane above (up)
.br
     \fB\-d\fR [HEIGHT[%]]    Split pane below (down)
.br
     \fB\-l\fR [WIDTH[%]]     Split pane left
.br
     \fB\-r\fR [WIDTH[%]]     Split pane right
.br

.br
e.g.
     \fB# Run in a popup covering 60% of the screen with the fcd layout at the bottom
     fcd \-\-tmux \-p 60% \-\- \-\-layout=bottom
     
     # Run in a bottom split with no border and path position set to header
     fcd \-\-tmux \-d 40% \-\-border=none \-\-path-position=header\fR
.br

.TP
.BI "\-\-popup" "[=[center|top|bottom|left|right][,SIZE[%]][,SIZE[%]][,border\-native]]"
Start  fcd in a tmux or Zellij floating pane (default \fBcenter,50%\fR). Requires
tmux 3.3+ or Zellij 0.44+. This option is ignored if fcd is  not  running  inside
tmux or Zellij.

On  tmux 3.7 or above and on Zellij, the floating pane is not modal; you can
switch to other panes and windows while fcd is running, and move and  resize
the pane with the mouse.

The first value specifies the popup position. The following values specify
the popup width and height. The
.B border\-native
option enables the native border style.

e.g.
     \fB# Popup in the center with 70% width and height
     fcd \-\-popup 70%

     # Popup on the right with 40% width and 100% height
     fcd \-\-popup right,40%

     # Popup on the bottom with 100% width and 30% height
     fcd \-\-popup bottom,30%

     # Popup on the top with 80% width and 40% height
     fcd \-\-popup top,80%,40%

     # Popup with a native tmux or Zellij border in the center with 80% width and height
     fcd \-\-popup center,80%,border\-native\fR

.SS OTHERS
.TP
.B "\-\-no\-mouse"
Disable mouse interaction
.TP
.B "\-\-ignore\-ctrl\-z"
Disable the Ctrl\-Z key binding inside the finder
.TP
.BI "\-\-query=" "STR"
Start the finder with the given query

.SS HELP
.TP
.B "\-\-version"
Display version information and exit
.TP
.B "\-\-help"
Show help message
.TP
.B "\-\-man"
Show man page

.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
.TP
.B FCD_DEFAULT_OPTS
Default options.
.br
e.g. \fBexport FCD_DEFAULT_OPTS="\-\-layout=top\-list \-\-border=dashed \-\-cycle"\fR
.TP
.B FCD_DEFAULT_OPTS_FILE
The location of the file that contains the default options.
.br
e.g. \fBexport FCD_DEFAULT_OPTS_FILE=~/.fcdrc\fR
.TP
.B FCD_BOOKMARKS_FILE
The location of the file that contains the bookmarks. When unset, the default locations
described in the
.BR BOOKMARKS
section are used.
.br
e.g. \fBexport FCD_BOOKMARKS_FILE=~/.config/fcd/bookmarks\fR

.SH EXIT STATUS
.BR 0 "      Normal exit"
.br
.BR 1 "      No match"
.br
.BR 2 "      Error"
.br
.BR 64 "     Starting directory selected; the status is returned even if it was printed"
.br
.BR 130 "    Interrupted with \fBCTRL\-C\fR or \fBESC\fR"

Other non-zero statuses returned by the underlying fzf process are propagated by fcd.

.SH BOOKMARKS
Bookmarks are stored one per line in
.B <name>=<path>
format.

Empty lines and lines beginning with
.B #
are ignored. Lines containing
.B =
are interpreted as bookmark entries, with the text before the first
.B =
as the name and the remaining text as the path.

The bookmark file is selected by
.BR FCD_BOOKMARKS_FILE .
When that variable is not set, fcd uses the following order:

.br
    1. \fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fcd/bookmarks\fR when \fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR is set.
.br
    2. \fB$HOME/.config/fcd/bookmarks\fR when \fB$HOME/.config\fR exists.
.br
    3. \fB$HOME/.fcd/bookmarks\fR otherwise.

Bookmarks can be saved with
.B Ctrl\-N
in any mode, and removed with
.BR Shift\-Delete
in bookmark mode.

.SH KEY BINDINGS
.TP
.B Enter
Select the highlighted target and exit
.TP
.B Left Arrow
Navigate to the parent directory or, in drive and bookmark mode, to the
corresponding parent navigation level
.TP
.B Alt\-Left
Navigate backward through directory navigation history
.TP
.B Right Arrow
Open the highlighted directory and continue navigation into it
.TP
.B Alt\-S
Switch to directory navigation mode
.TP
.B Alt\-Q
Switch to drive navigation mode
.TP
.B Alt\-N
Switch to bookmark navigation mode
.TP
.B Ctrl\-N
Save the selected path as a bookmark. In directory mode the highlighted
entry is used as the bookmark name. The bookmark file and its parent directory
are created when necessary.
.TP
.B Shift\-Delete
Delete the highlighted bookmark while in bookmark mode
.TP
.B Ctrl\-H
Toggle hidden directories in the navigation list
.TP
.B Alt\-H
Toggle hidden directories and files in the preview
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Y
Copy the current navigation path to the clipboard
.TP
.B Alt\-Y
Copy the full path of the highlighted item to the clipboard
.TP
.B Ctrl\-P
Toggle the preview window
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Home
Jump to the home directory in directory mode. In drive and bookmark modes,
reset navigation to the root of the current selected target.
.TP
.B Alt\-Home
Reset navigation to the starting point of the current navigation mode. In
directory mode this is the directory from which \fBfcd\fR was started; in drive
and bookmark modes this returns to the top-level list.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-R
Reload the current directory, drive list, or bookmark list
.PP
Most standard fzf key bindings remain available where they are not overridden by fcd.

.SH DEPENDENCIES
.B fcd
requires
.BR bash ,
.BR fzf ,
and
.BR find .

The following commands provide optional functionality:

.br
.BR tree (1)
for tree-style previews;
.br
.BR wl\-copy ,
.BR xclip ,
or
.BR xsel
for clipboard integration;
.br
.BR lsblk (8)
for drive and partition detection; and
.br
.BR sort (1)
for sorting.

